What is the TCP/IP Model?
The TCP/IP Model is a set of rules that allows computers to communicate over a network.
Think of it as the language of the Internet.
If two computers follow these rules, they can exchange data without any problem.

Why Do We Need TCP/IP?

Imagine everyone spoke a different language.

One person speaks English.
Another speaks Kannada.
Another speaks Japanese.

Communication would be difficult.

Similarly, computers also need common rules.

TCP/IP provides those rules so every device connected to the Internet can communicate.

Why is it Called TCP/IP?

The name comes from two important protocols:

TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)
Makes sure data reaches the destination correctly.
If some data is lost, it sends it again.
Reliable communication.

Example:

Downloading a PDF file.

You want every page to arrive correctly.

IP (Internet Protocol)

IP is responsible for finding the destination.

Every device has an IP Address.

IP decides where the packet should go.

Example:

Sending a letter.

The address on the envelope tells the post office where to deliver it.

IP works the same way.

TCP/IP Layers

Unlike the OSI Model (7 layers), TCP/IP has only 4 layers.
+----------------------+
|  Application Layer   |
+----------------------+
|   Transport Layer    |
+----------------------+
|    Internet Layer    |
+----------------------+
| Network Access Layer |
+----------------------+

1. Application Layer

This is where users interact with the network.

Examples:

Chrome
Firefox
Gmail
Outlook
WhatsApp

Common Protocols:

HTTP
HTTPS
FTP
SMTP
DNS

Example:

Opening YouTube starts at the Application Layer.

2. Transport Layer

This layer is responsible for delivering data between applications.

It uses:

TCP
UDP

Responsibilities:

Reliable communication
Error checking
Port numbers

Example:

When downloading a movie, TCP ensures all parts arrive correctly.

3. Internet Layer

This layer is responsible for routing packets to the correct destination.

It uses:

IP Address

Main Protocols:

IPv4
IPv6
ICMP

Example:

When you open google.com, the packet travels to Google's IP address through routers.

4. Network Access Layer

This is the lowest layer.

It handles communication between your device and the local network.

It includes:

Ethernet
Wi-Fi
MAC Address

Example:

Your laptop sends data through Wi-Fi to your router.

How Data Travels

Suppose you open www.youtube.com.
Chrome Browser
        │
        ▼
Application Layer
        │
        ▼
Transport Layer (TCP)
        │
        ▼
Internet Layer (IP)
        │
        ▼
Network Access Layer (Wi-Fi)
        │
        ▼
Internet
        │
        ▼
YouTube Server


The server sends the response back using the same layers in reverse.

OSI vs TCP/IP
| OSI Model                                | TCP/IP Model         |
| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------- |
| 7 Layers                                 | 4 Layers             |
| Learning model                           | Practical model      |
| Mostly used for understanding networking | Used on the Internet |
| More detailed                            | Simpler              |

